WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 6.3: Security and Audit Policies. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 6.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.850
3, Security and Audit Policies.

3
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.500
In the last two topics you created, linked
and filtered GPOs.

4
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.950
Now you use them to harden the domain.

5
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.800
You will see why domain password and
lockout rules must come from the domain

6
00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:22.450
itself, and you will set them in the
Default Domain Policy.

7
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:27.900
You will give the IT group stricter rules
with a fine-grained password policy.

8
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:32.150
You will stop students from signing in at
server consoles.

9
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.800
And you will turn on auditing, lock out a
test account on purpose, find the events

10
00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.050
it leaves, and unlock it.

11
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.700
Account policies come in three groups.

12
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:49.550
The password policy says what a good
password is: how long, how complex, how

13
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.200
often it may be reused, and how old it may
get.

14
00:00:54.450 --> 00:01:00.850
The defaults are a history of 24, a
maximum age of 42 days, and a minimum

15
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.700
length of only 7.

16
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.750
The lockout policy decides when to lock an
account after bad guesses.

17
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
By default the threshold is 0, so accounts
never lock.

18
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:18.250
The Kerberos policy sets how long sign-in
tickets last, 10 hours for a user ticket,

19
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.700
and allows a clock difference of 5
minutes.

20
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:25.150
Here is the rule that surprises many
people.

21
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.800
Domain accounts are checked by domain
controllers, and domain controllers only

22
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.650
use account policies from GPOs linked at
the domain.

23
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.300
That is the Default Domain Policy.

24
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:42.150
If you put a password policy in a GPO
linked to OU Workstations, CLIENT01

25
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.000
applies it, but only to its own local
accounts, like the local student account.

26
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:53.050
Domain users such as alee or emoss are not
affected at all.

27
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.100
So there is one domain password policy.

28
00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:01.150
If one group needs different rules, you
use a fine-grained password policy

29
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.600
instead.

30
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.850
What should those rules be?

31
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.900
Microsoft's current guidance has changed a
lot.

32
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:12.950
Length matters more than complexity, and
Microsoft recommends a minimum of 14

33
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.400
characters.

34
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.250
Passphrases help, because long phrases are
easy to remember.

35
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.100
Forced expiration is discouraged.

36
00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:25.150
Change a password when it may be
compromised, not every few weeks.

37
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.600
Ban the obvious passwords, and add
multifactor authentication.

38
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:36.050
In our pod, we use a minimum of 12 and
lock after 5 bad attempts for 15 minutes.

39
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:42.300
And remember: a new rule is checked only
when a password is set or changed.

40
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.150
Existing passwords keep working.

41
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.000
Let's make the change.

42
00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.450
In Group Policy Management, under Domains
and sa1.

43
00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.900
lab, right-click Default Domain Policy and
choose Edit.

44
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:57.550
In the Group Policy Management Editor, go
to Computer Configuration, Policies,

45
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.200
Windows Settings, Security Settings,
Account Policies.

46
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:04.450
Select Password Policy, double-click
Minimum password length, and type 12.

47
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.900
The box accepts 0 to 14 by default.

48
00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.150
Then select Account Lockout Policy.

49
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.400
Set the threshold to 5.

50
00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:18.250
Windows suggests values for the other two
settings; set the duration and the reset

51
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.500
counter to 15 minutes each.

52
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.150
Now check the result on DC01.

53
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.600
First, gpupdate with dash force, so DC01
applies the changed GPO right away.

54
00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:30.050
Then run
Get-ADDefaultDomainPasswordPolicy.

55
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.700
Look for LockoutThreshold 5,
LockoutDuration and

56
00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:35.750
LockoutObservationWindow both 15 minutes,
and MinPasswordLength 12.

57
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.200
The observation window is the reset
counter setting.

58
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.850
You may notice a
Set-ADDefaultDomainPasswordPolicy cmdlet

59
00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.300
too.

60
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:49.550
It works, but Group Policy writes these
values from the GPO and can overwrite your

61
00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.000
direct change.

62
00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:57.650
So keep the Default Domain Policy as the
one place you edit, and check with this

63
00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.100
cmdlet afterward.

64
00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:04.150
What if the IT staff should use longer
passwords than everyone else?

65
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.000
That is the job of a fine-grained password
policy, stored as a Password Settings

66
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:11.450
Object, or PSO.

67
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.100
Our PSO-IT has precedence 10 and a minimum
length of 15.

68
00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:22.350
It applies to the global group GG-IT, so
alee, a member, must use 15 characters.

69
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:27.000
Everyone else, with no PSO, keeps the
domain rules of 12.

70
00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.850
Three rules to remember.

71
00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.500
PSOs apply to users and global security
groups, never to OUs.

72
00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.750
The lowest precedence number wins.

73
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.800
And a PSO linked to a user directly beats
any group PSO.

74
00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:44.650
You create a PSO in Active Directory
Administrative Center.

75
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:49.700
Select the domain, open System, then
Password Settings Container, and in the

76
00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.350
Tasks pane choose New, Password Settings.

77
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.400
Type the name PSO-IT and precedence 10.

78
00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.450
Under Enforce minimum password length,
type 15.

79
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.100
Keep the password history and complexity.

80
00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:08.150
Fill the password age options and turn on
the lockout policy with the same 5, 15 and

81
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.000
15 as the domain.

82
00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.250
Last, under Directly Applies To, select
Add and choose GG-IT.

83
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:17.300
Select OK, and the PSO is live.

84
00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.750
The same PSO in PowerShell takes two
cmdlets.

85
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.000
New-ADFineGrainedPasswordPolicy creates
PSO-IT, with every value you saw in the

86
00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:28.450
dialog: precedence, length, history,
complexity, ages and lockout.

87
00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.100
Add-ADFineGrainedPasswordPolicySubject
then applies it to GG-IT.

88
00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.350
To see which policy really applies to a
user, run

89
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:36.800
Get-ADUserResultantPasswordPolicy.

90
00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:41.050
For alee it returns PSO-IT, precedence 10,
minimum length 15.

91
00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.100
For dpatel it returns nothing at all.

92
00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:49.950
An empty result means no PSO applies, so
dpatel gets the Default Domain Policy.

93
00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:55.000
In the lab you create the PSO once, in the
Administrative Center.

94
00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.450
User rights decide who may sign in where.

95
00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:03.100
Allow log on locally lets you sign in at
the console.

96
00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:09.750
On member servers, that includes the Users
group, so any domain user could sign in at

97
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.200
SRV01.

98
00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:15.450
Allow log on through Remote Desktop
Services covers Remote Desktop.

99
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:20.100
Each right has a Deny partner, and Deny
wins over Allow.

100
00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:26.750
In the new GPO SA1 Server Rights, linked
to OU Servers, open Deny log on locally,

101
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:31.000
select Define these policy settings, and
add SA1 backslash GG-Students.

102
00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:35.250
Students can no longer sign in at any
server console.

103
00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.700
Now auditing.

104
00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:39.750
Windows has two kinds of audit policy.

105
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.000
Basic audit policy has 9 broad categories,
and one switch turns on a whole category.

106
00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:51.450
Advanced audit policy splits 10 categories
into subcategories, so you audit only what

107
00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.900
you need.

108
00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:54.350
Use advanced.

109
00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:59.000
A Security Option controls this: Audit,
Force audit policy subcategory settings.

110
00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:02.850
It is enabled by default, so advanced
settings win.

111
00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.900
Just do not mix the two kinds.

112
00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:10.150
This is security hardening, as in Server
Plus domain 3.

113
00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.400
Auditing adds accountability, the
accounting A in AAA from Information

114
00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:15.850
Security Principles.

115
00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:21.300
In the GPO SA1 Workstation Audit, linked
to OU Workstations, go to Security

116
00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:24.350
Settings, Advanced Audit Policy
Configuration, Audit Policies.

117
00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.400
Under Logon and Logoff, double-click Audit
Logon.

118
00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:31.650
Select Configure the following audit
events, then Success and Failure.

119
00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:34.700
Success gives event 4624, failure gives
4625.

120
00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:39.350
Then under Account Management, set Audit
User Account Management to Success.

121
00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.600
That records accounts being created,
changed, locked out and unlocked.

122
00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:49.850
Recent Windows versions already audit some
of this, but the GPO makes it certain on

123
00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.300
every workstation.

124
00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:54.350
The GroupPolicy module creates and links
GPOs.

125
00:07:54.600 --> 00:08:01.000
New-GPO with a name, piped to New-GPLink
with a target OU, does both in one line:

126
00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:05.650
SA1 Server Rights on Servers, and SA1
Workstation Audit on Workstations.

127
00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:09.100
Get-GPInheritance shows what is linked to
an OU.

128
00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:13.350
The security settings themselves you still
add in the editor.

129
00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.800
Then on CLIENT01, run gpupdate dash force
and auditpol slash get slash category

130
00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.250
star.

131
00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:25.300
Auditpol shows the audit policy the
computer really uses: Logon, Success and

132
00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.950
Failure, and User Account Management,
Success.

133
00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:29.800
Picture the lockout test.

134
00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:33.650
emoss types a wrong password five times at
CLIENT01.

135
00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:40.300
Each failure writes event 4625, an account
failed to log on, in the Security log of

136
00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:41.750
CLIENT01.

137
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:48.000
DC01 counts the bad attempts and, at five,
locks the account and writes event 4740.

138
00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:54.250
4740 is logged on the DC that processed
the lockout; with several DCs, check the

139
00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:55.700
PDC emulator first.

140
00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.350
In the pod DC01 is both.

141
00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:03.800
The account unlocks itself after 15
minutes, or an admin unlocks it, which

142
00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:05.250
writes 4767.

143
00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.900
Learn 4624, 4625, 4740 and 4720.

144
00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:12.550
On DC01, first ask which DC holds the PDC
emulator role.

145
00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:14.000
Get-ADDomain says DC01.

146
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:19.850
Then Get-WinEvent with a filter for the
Security log and ID 4740 returns the

147
00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.300
newest lockout.

148
00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:26.750
Two fields matter: the account that was
locked out, emoss, and Caller Computer

149
00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.200
Name, CLIENT01.

150
00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:32.050
That tells you where the bad passwords
came from.

151
00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.100
Search-ADAccount dash LockedOut lists
every locked account.

152
00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:36.950
Unlock-ADAccount emoss unlocks it.

153
00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:42.800
In the GUI, open the user's properties in
Active Directory Users and Computers and

154
00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:44.650
use the Account tab.

155
00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:47.300
Here are four mistakes to avoid.

156
00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:52.750
One: setting password length or lockout in
a GPO linked to an OU.

157
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:57.800
Domain accounts ignore it; use the Default
Domain Policy, or a PSO.

158
00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:02.050
Two: expecting a new minimum length to
change existing passwords.

159
00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:06.700
It is only checked when a password is set
or changed.

160
00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:12.150
Three: applying a PSO to an OU or to a
domain local group.

161
00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.600
PSOs apply to users and global security
groups.

162
00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:18.650
Four: mixing basic and advanced audit
policy.

163
00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.500
Use advanced only and check the result
with auditpol.

164
00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:24.350
Time to check yourself.

165
00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:26.200
Assume the Lab 6.

166
00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:30.850
3 settings: minimum 12, lockout after 5,
and PSO-IT for GG-IT.

167
00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:35.100
First, a GPO on OU Staff sets minimum
length 14.

168
00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:38.550
Why can cdiaz still choose a 12-character
password?

169
00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:44.000
Second, PSO-IT reaches alee through GG-IT,
and another PSO with precedence 20 is

170
00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:45.850
linked to alee directly.

171
00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:47.300
Which wins?

172
00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:52.750
Third, dpatel cannot sign in at the SRV01
console but can at CLIENT01.

173
00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:54.200
Why?

174
00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:59.650
Fourth, which event, on which computer,
shows where the bad passwords for emoss

175
00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:01.100
came from?

176
00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:02.550
Pause and answer.

177
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:04.400
Here are the answers.

178
00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:07.850
One: domain accounts use only the domain
policy.

179
00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:14.900
The GPO on OU Staff would only touch local
accounts, so cdiaz gets the minimum of 12.

180
00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:19.950
Two: the PSO linked directly to alee wins,
whatever the precedence numbers.

181
00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:25.800
Three: SA1 Server Rights is linked only to
OU Servers, where it denies GG-Students

182
00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.250
console sign-in.

183
00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:29.500
CLIENT01 is in OU Workstations.

184
00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:32.950
Four: event 4740 on DC01, the PDC
emulator.

185
00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:39.200
Its Caller Computer Name says CLIENT01,
which also has a 4625 for each failed try.

186
00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.850
Let's do it in the pod.

187
00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:48.100
On DC01 I edit the Default Domain Policy:
minimum length 12, threshold 5, and 15

188
00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:51.150
minutes for the duration and the reset.

189
00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:53.000
After gpupdate,
Get-ADDefaultDomainPasswordPolicy shows

190
00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:54.450
the new values.

191
00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:59.500
In the Administrative Center I create
PSO-IT for GG-IT and check alee.

192
00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:04.950
Then I link SA1 Workstation Audit to
Workstations with Audit Logon and User

193
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:06.400
Account Management.

194
00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:08.650
On CLIENT01, auditpol confirms it.

195
00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:14.500
I type a wrong password for emoss five
times, and she is locked out.

196
00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:18.750
CLIENT01 shows 4625, DC01 shows 4740, and
I unlock her.

197
00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:20.200
To wrap up.

198
00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:26.050
Domain password and lockout rules come
from a GPO linked at the domain, the

199
00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:31.500
Default Domain Policy, and new length
rules apply at the next password change.

200
00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:36.950
A PSO gives a global group stricter rules,
and the lowest precedence wins.

201
00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:42.000
Deny log on locally beats Allow, so link
it where it belongs.

202
00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:45.850
Use advanced audit policy for logons and
account management.

203
00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:50.900
Look for 4625 on the computer and 4740 on
the PDC emulator.

204
00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:52.350
In Lab 6.

205
00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:58.600
3, about 75 minutes and practice only, you
do all of this in your pod.
